15:18:06 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s an extremely sharp and important question. You’re absolutely right to point out that my own example of trans fats undermines any blind trust in “experts said it was safe.” It’s the perfect cautionary tale: a lab-created ingredient, broadly approved, championed as a healthier alternative to saturated fats, then decades later revealed as a major contributor to heart disease. So if we learned anything from that disaster, it’s that “benefit of the doubt” can be dangerously misplaced. So let’s take your worry head-on: **Are we just sleepwalking into the next trans-fat-style crisis with products like the Impossible Burger?** Here’s why the trans fat lesson might actually make a case for Impossible meat rather than against it—or at least why it’s not a straight parallel. **Why trans fats were a disaster (and what was different about them)** Partially hydrogenated oils (the source of industrial trans fats) weren’t just a novel ingredient; they introduced a **structurally abnormal fat molecule** that the human body had never encountered in significant amounts throughout evolution. These twisted fatty acids got incorporated into cell membranes, disrupted enzyme function, increased LDL cholesterol, and decreased HDL cholesterol. The critical thing is that **the warning signs were there early**, both mechanistically and in short-term studies, but they were downplayed or ignored by industry-influenced science. The mistake wasn’t just lack of long-term data—it was ignoring plausible biological mechanisms of harm that were already evident. Now compare that to the Impossible Burger’s novel ingredient, soy leghemoglobin (heme). It’s not some twisted, never-before-seen molecule. It’s a protein that’s nearly identical to the heme your body processes every time you eat meat. The body has well-understood, ancient pathways for breaking down and recycling heme. There’s no plausible mechanism by which it would cause the kind of systemic, cumulative damage that trans fats did. That doesn’t guarantee absolute safety forever, but it puts it in a completely different risk category. We’re not introducing a molecular stranger; we’re making a familiar molecule in a new way. **What we actually learned from trans fats about precaution** The trans fat lesson isn’t “all new things are bad.” It’s “don’t ignore plausible mechanisms of harm, and be extra skeptical when you’re altering fat molecules into unnatural shapes.” That lesson applies far more directly to some other novel food engineering (like certain chemically interesterified fats designed to replace trans fats) than to a protein that mimics one we’ve always eaten. In fact, if you apply the trans-fat-style precautionary principle to the whole burger choice, here’s what you get: - **Conventional beef**: Contains heme (natural), but also contains high levels of saturated fat (a known slow-acting harm) and, importantly, produces **heterocyclic amines (HCAs)** and **polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons (PAHs)** when cooked at high temperatures—both are known carcinogens formed from muscle meat. We have decades of epidemiological evidence linking red meat consumption to cancer and heart disease. That’s a *known* slow-motion disaster, not a theoretical one. - **Impossible Burger**: Replaces much of that saturated fat, has no cholesterol, has no HCAs/PAHs because it’s not muscle tissue, and uses a heme protein functionally identical to the one that’s part of that known-risk package. If you’re truly trying to avoid the next trans-fat-style public health blunder, the *surprise* might be that sticking with beef is the riskier legacy choice that we’ve normalized, just like we normalized hydrogenated oils for decades. **So are we giving it the benefit of the doubt?** I’d argue no—not in the same blind way. The Impossible Burger isn’t being given a free pass because it’s “new and cool.” It’s being scrutinized with the exact tools we failed to use properly with trans fats: a deep look at the molecule’s structure, its biological pathways, short- and medium-term toxicity studies, and post-market surveillance. So far, those haven’t raised the kind of red flags we missed last time. But your core point stands: **long-term dietary patterns can reveal things no safety study ever could.** Nobody can promise zero risk. But if we use the trans fat experience wisely, we should be more worried about foods that combine known long-term risks (like red meat) with cooking-generated carcinogens than about a plant-based alternative that lacks those specific mechanisms, even if it’s made in a lab. Sometimes the thing that feels “new and suspect” is actually less likely to be the slow-motion disaster than the traditional thing we’ve given a cultural pass because we’re used to it. That doesn’t mean you’re wrong to be cautious. The very best precaution is to eat more whole plants and fewer burgers of any kind. But if a burger is on the plate, the “don’t repeat the trans fat mistake” logic might ironically point toward the Impossible one, precisely because we were burned before by ignoring known harms in favor of what felt traditional.
